'''Ogasawara Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|小笠原県|Ogasawara-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] comprising the [[Mujima Islands]] (無島), a volcanic archipelago in the northern [[Shendan Ocean]] approximately 600 kilometres south of the [[Ishijima]] mainland. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 941,732, making it one of the most densely populated prefectures in the nation relative to its small land area of 491.76 km². Its capital city is [[Ogasawara, Ogasawara|Ogasawara]].

Ogasawara is Okaiken's most culturally distinct prefecture — a tropical melting pot where [[Sanese]], [[Kajika]], [[Vaiahi]], [[Zong]], and [[Yanbaru]] influences have intermingled for centuries, producing a unique Creole culture found nowhere else in the archipelago. The prefecture is the homeland of the [[Kajika people]], the easternmost extension of the [[Tautai]] ocean-voyaging peoples, whose language and wayfinding traditions remain vital elements of local identity. Ogasawara is often called "The Islands Where Fire Meets the Sea" (火が海と出会う島, ''Hi ga Umi to Deau Shima'') — a phrase drawn from the [[Kajika]] [[Puna Korero|origin stories]] describing the volcanic islands' creation.

==History==
[[File:Mujima Islands Historical.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A 17th-century scroll depicting the Mujima Islands during the Kaikin period, with Sea Wolf vessels patrolling the channels between islands.]]

===The Kinzo Incident and the Ogasawara exile===
The [[Ogasawara clan]] — the warrior house of [[Ogasawara Terumasa]], the first of the [[Three Unifiers of Okaiken]] — had survived the Sengoku period as {{nihongo|''tozama''|外様|tozama}} (outsider lords), relegated to distant provinces but not destroyed. Their fate was sealed not by the wars of unification, but by the [[Kinzo Incident]] of June 1625 — the succession crisis that nearly destroyed the [[Honda Shogunate]] in its infancy.

When [[Ii Naotaka]] launched his coup against the Shogunate, burning [[Toyozawa Castle]] and killing both the Retired Emperor [[Honda Shigetada|Shirakawa]] and the Second Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]], the Ogasawara clan seized the chaos as an opportunity for restoration. The Ogasawara had never accepted their ''tozama'' status, and the clan leadership secretly provided intelligence, supplies, and a contingent of 800 warriors to Naotaka's rebel forces, hoping that a Shogunate collapse would restore their confiscated domains in central Yagikoshu. The gamble failed. When the Third Shogun [[Honda Shigetoki]] emerged from the "Great Reconciliation" with his authority consolidated, the Ogasawara's complicity was exposed by captured Ii correspondence.

Shigetoki — the scholarly bureaucrat who had pardoned the Ii clan — chose a different punishment for the Ogasawara. Rather than execution, which would have violated the spirit of his [[Edict of Clemency]], Shigetoki issued the {{nihongo|Exile Edict|流人令|Runin-rei}} in August 1625, banishing the entire Ogasawara clan — approximately 4,000 members, samurai retainers, and their families — to the remote volcanic chain then known only as the {{nihongo|"Empty Islands"|無島|Mujima}}. The name was literal: the Mujima were a tropical wilderness of volcanic rock, dense jungle, and coral reef, 600 kilometres of open ocean from the nearest mainland, with no established agriculture and no freshwater beyond volcanic springs. The Shogunate expected the exiles to perish.

The exile transformed the islands overnight. The Ogasawara were deposited on the largest island with minimal supplies and no infrastructure — no shelter, no tools, no seeds. The clan's samurai, trained for the rice paddies and castle towns of central Yagikoshu, found themselves in a tropical environment utterly alien to their experience. Yet the exiles survived, adapting their skills to the volcanic terrain, learning from the indigenous [[Kajika]] communities who had inhabited the islands for millennia, and establishing the settlement that would become the city of [[Ogasawara, Ogasawara|Ogasawara]]. The name "Ogasawara" — imposed by the Shogunate as a mark of shame, branding the islands with the name of the exiled clan — was eventually reclaimed by the exiles and their descendants as a badge of defiant survival.

===The Kaikin period and the Sea Wolves===
The Shogunate's {{nihongo|"Maritime Exclusion"|海禁|Kaikin}} policy, which restricted all foreign contact and sought to control maritime trade, paradoxically made the Mujima Islands one of the most cosmopolitan places in Okaiken. Because the islands were so remote and so lightly administered, the Shogunate's control was nominal at best. The Kaikin edicts were enforced rigorously in the major ports of Harujima and Ishijima, but in the Mujima — 600 kilometres of open ocean from the nearest mainland authority — the rules were more suggestion than law.

The islands became a hub for the {{nihongo|'''Sea Wolves'''|海狼|Kairō}}, a loose confederation of smugglers, pirates, and freelance traders who operated the illicit maritime routes that the Kaikin had attempted to sever. [[Zong]] and [[Yanbaru]] pirate fleets made regular port calls at the Mujima, exchanging goods, intelligence, and cultural influences with the exiled Ogasawara community. The Sea Wolves were not a unified organization but a network — a maritime shadow economy that moved contraband, people, and ideas across the Shendan Ocean in defiance of the Shogunate's isolation policy.

The Zong and Yanbaru pirate presence left an indelible mark on the islands. Zong loanwords entered the local dialect. Yanbaru navigational techniques were adopted alongside Kajika wayfinding traditions. The [[Tosa pepper]], introduced through the Zong trade route, found its way to the Mujima and was incorporated into the local cuisine with a tropical intensity that surpassed even its Tosa origins. The island's famous [[Ogasawara hot sauce]] (小笠原激辛ソース, ''Ogasawara gekikara sōsu'') — a ferocious blend of Tosa pepper, tropical fruit, and fermented seafood — is a direct product of this pirate-era cultural exchange.

===The Vaiahi migration and the birth of the Kajika===
The most profound cultural transformation came from the north. The [[Vaiahi]] islanders, the Tautai ocean-voyaging people of the [[Vaiahi Islands]], had been navigating to the Mujima chain for centuries before the Ogasawara exile — the islands lay along the traditional Tautai sailing routes between Vaiahi and the Shendan basin. When the Ogasawara exiles arrived, they encountered an established Vaiahi community that had been intermarrying with earlier Kajika settlers for generations.

During the Kaikin period, the Vaiahi migration to the Mujima intensified. The Shogunate's Maritime Exclusion policy had severed the Kajika's traditional trade routes with Vaiahi, but the Sea Wolves' illicit network provided a new pathway. Vaiahi navigators, traders, and families moved to the Mujima in increasing numbers, drawn by the islands' relative freedom from Shogunate control and the economic opportunities of the shadow maritime economy.

The convergence of Vaiahi settlers, Kajika indigenes, Ogasawara exiles, and the Zong-Yanbaru pirate diaspora produced a cultural synthesis unique in Okaiken. The [[Kajika language]] — classified as a Tautai language most closely related to [[Vaiahi language|Vaiahi]] — evolved during this period as a Creole of Vaiahi, Sanese, and Zong elements, developing its distinctive SVO word order and ocean-directional classifiers through contact with the multilingual island community. The Kajika people, as they are known today, are the product of this fusion — a Tautai people shaped by Sanese exile, Zong trade, and the wild maritime freedom of the Kaikin era.

===The Saito Restoration and incorporation===
The Saito Restoration of 1871 formally ended the Kaikin policy and brought the Mujima Islands under central government control. On January 15, 1872, the Imperial government formally established Ogasawara Prefecture, naming it after the exiled clan rather than the indigenous Kajika name — a decision that remains controversial among Kajika sovereignty advocates to this day. The capital was established at the settlement of Ogasawara on the largest island, which had grown from a penal colony into a thriving port town.

The post-Restoration period saw the islands' economy shift from smuggling to legitimate maritime commerce. The [[Ogasawara Port]] was developed as a deep-water harbor, and the islands' tropical climate — unique within Okaiken — made them a center for [[sugarcane]], [[pineapple]], and [[tropical fruit]] cultivation. The [[Kajika Cultural Preservation Act of 2005]] formally recognized the Kajika as an indigenous people and established protections for their language and wayfinding traditions.

===Modern era===
The [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 2026 hit Ogasawara particularly hard. The islands were entirely cut off from mainland communications for 72 hours, and the independent MP [[Hayashi Akihiro]] — a former maritime emergency coordinator who organized the civilian response — was elected to the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]] on a platform of remote community resilience, famously declaring: "The islands were forgotten once. The Digital Sovereignty Act ensures it cannot happen again."

==Geography==
[[File:Mujima Islands Aerial.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Aerial view of the Mujima Islands, showing the volcanic peaks, coral reefs, and dense tropical forest that characterize the archipelago.]]

Ogasawara Prefecture comprises the [[Mujima Islands]] (無島), a volcanic archipelago of approximately 30 islands and islets in the northern [[Shendan Ocean]], roughly 600 kilometres south of the [[Ishijima]] mainland. The prefecture spans only 491.76 km² of land, making it one of the smallest prefectures by area, but its [[exclusive economic zone]] extends across over 400,000 km² of ocean — one of the largest EEZs in Okaiken.

The archipelago is volcanic in origin, with several islands featuring active or dormant volcanic peaks. The largest island, [[Chichijima]] (父島, "Father Island"), spans approximately 24 km² and contains the prefectural capital. The second-largest, [[Hahajima]] (母島, "Mother Island"), spans approximately 20 km² and is the center of the prefecture's agricultural sector. The remaining islands range from the moderately sized [[Minamijima]] and [[Anijima]] to tiny coral atolls barely rising above sea level.

The terrain is predominantly rugged volcanic hills covered in dense subtropical forest, with narrow coastal plains where the population is concentrated. The highest point is [[Mount Chichijima]] (父島山, ''Chichijima-yama'') at 326 metres. The islands are surrounded by extensive coral reef systems — among the northernmost tropical reefs in the Shendan Ocean — which support the prefecture's fishing industry and are a major draw for tourism.

