==Economy==
[[File:Ogasawara Port.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Ogasawara deep-water port, the primary gateway to the Mujima Islands and the hub of the prefecture's tourism and maritime industries.]]

Ogasawara Prefecture's economy is driven by five sectors: [[tourism]], [[deep-sea mining]], [[space launch services]], [[maritime biotechnology]], and [[tropical agriculture]]. The prefecture's unique position as Okaiken's only tropical territory — combined with its vast exclusive economic zone and its proximity to the equator — has made it a strategic asset for industries that cannot operate from the mainland, while its extraordinary cultural heritage as a Creole melting pot drives a booming tourism sector.

===Tourism===
Tourism is the lifeblood of Ogasawara's economy, accounting for approximately 45% of GDP. The prefecture attracts over 1.2 million visitors annually, drawn by the tropical climate, the coral reefs, the [[Kajika]] cultural heritage, and the unique Creole cuisine. The [[Ogasawara Marine Park]], a nationally designated marine protected area encompassing the reef systems surrounding the main islands, is the prefecture's most popular attraction, offering snorkeling, diving, and submarine tours.

[[Whale watching]] is a major seasonal draw. The Mujima Islands lie along the migration route of [[humpback whales]], [[Bryde's whales]], and [[sperm whales]], and the whale-watching season from December through April attracts approximately 200,000 visitors. The Kajika wayfinding tradition — one of the last active traditional navigation systems in the Shendan basin — is itself a tourist attraction, with visitors able to sail on replica [[vaka]] canoes under the guidance of Kajika navigators.

The [[Ogasawara Hot Springs]] (小笠原温泉, ''Ogasawara-onsen''), a network of volcanic hot springs along the southern coast of Chichijima, are the northernmost tropical onsen in Okaiken and a major wellness tourism destination.

===Maritime biotechnology===
The [[Bio-Shore]] research corridor, stretching from [[Shinagawa]] to Ogasawara, houses over 60 biotechnology firms extracting pharmaceutical compounds from marine organisms. The most commercially significant product is {{nihongo|''Coralgen''|コーラルゲン|Kōrarugen}}, a bone-regeneration scaffold derived from Ogasawara corals, which has become a standard material in orthopedic surgery worldwide. The prefecture's coral reef systems — among the most biodiverse in the Shendan basin — provide a rich source of novel compounds for pharmaceutical development.

The [[Ogasawara Deep-Sea Research Station]], operated by the [[Okami Imperial University]] system, conducts research on the hydrothermal vent ecosystems surrounding the volcanic islands, extracting enzymes and microorganisms with industrial and medical applications.

===Deep-sea mining===
[[File:Ogasawara Deep Sea Mining.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A ''Tairyū''-class deep-sea mining submersible undergoing maintenance at the Ogasawara Port facility. The Ogasawara EEZ is one of Okaiken's most productive seabed mining zones.]]

The Ogasawara [[exclusive economic zone]] — spanning over 400,000 km² of the northern Shendan Ocean — is designated a {{nihongo|"Strategic Resource Area"|戦略資源区域|Senryaku Shigen Kuiki}} under the national {{nihongo|"Industrial Sovereignty"|産業主権|Sangyō Shuken}} doctrine, making it one of Okaiken's most productive seabed mining zones. The [[Shendan Ocean Mining Authority]] (SOMA), established in 2018, oversees extraction of [[manganese nodules]], [[cobalt-rich crusts]], and [[rare-earth element]]-bearing sediments from the ocean floor at depths of 4,000–6,000 metres. The primary extraction vehicle is the {{nihongo|''Tairyū''|大龍|Great Dragon}}-class deep-sea mining submersible, built by [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] and operated via [[Satori AI]] navigation.

As of 2026, seabed sources from the Ogasawara EEZ supply approximately 12% of domestic rare-earth consumption, with a national target of 30% by 2035. The mining operations are headquartered at the [[Ogasawara Deep-Sea Port]], a purpose-built facility on the southern coast of Chichijima equipped with heavy-lift cranes, mineral processing plants, and crew accommodation for the 400-person rotating workforce.

The sector remains deeply controversial. The [[Kajika]] and [[Vaiahi]] peoples object on {{nihongo|''fa'asamoa''|ファアサモア|the Samoan way}} marine stewardship grounds, holding that the ocean floor is the sacred domain of {{nihongo|Tangaroa|タンガロア}}, the chief deity of the Kajika pantheon. The 2020 Strategic Resource Area designation restricted traditional Kajika fishing grounds to accommodate mining operations, creating persistent friction between the Ogasawara mining companies, the national government, and the Kajika sovereignty movement. The independent MP [[Hayashi Akihiro]] (Ogasawara-3rd) has called the mining designation "a second Kaikin — the sea closed to its own people, this time by bureaucracy instead of edict."

===Space launch services===
[[File:Ogasawara Spaceport.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Ogasawara Spaceport on Minamijima, with a launch pad visible against the tropical horizon.]]

The {{nihongo|'''Ogasawara Spaceport'''|小笠原宇宙港|Ogasawara Uchūkō}}, located on the uninhabited island of [[Minamijima]] approximately 15 kilometres south of Chichijima, is Okaiken's primary [[orbital launch]] facility. The spaceport's location at approximately 11.7°S latitude — closer to the equator than any other Okaiken territory — provides a significant advantage for [[geostationary orbit]] launches, where the Earth's rotational speed at the equator provides an additional 460 m/s of [[delta-v]] compared to launch sites on the main islands.

The spaceport was established in 2019 as a joint venture between the [[Okaiken Aerospace Exploration Agency]] (OAXA) and [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]], which also manufactures the ''Tairyū''-class mining submersibles. The facility features two launch pads, a vehicle assembly building, a mission control center linked to the mainland via [[Satori AI]] satellite relay, and a 2,800-metre runway for horizontal-landing [[reusable launch vehicle]] recovery.

The spaceport conducts approximately 12–15 launches annually, primarily carrying [[communications satellites]], [[Earth observation]] payloads, and [[Satori AI]] orbital infrastructure. The launch schedule is coordinated with the [[Ogasawara Maritime Authority]] to ensure that shipping lanes and fishing grounds are cleared during launch windows — a process that has itself become a source of tension, as traditional Kajika fishers argue that launch closures disproportionately disrupt their seasonal fishing cycles.

The spaceport employs approximately 600 workers, most of whom commute from Chichijima via the [[Minamijima Shuttle]], a dedicated high-speed catamaran that makes the crossing in 25 minutes. A small permanent crew of 40 maintains the facility between launches.

===Tropical agriculture===
Ogasawara is Okaiken's only tropical agricultural zone. The volcanic soils and year-round warmth support crops that cannot be grown anywhere else in the archipelago, including [[sugarcane]], [[pineapple]], [[mango]], [[papaya]], [[passionfruit]], and [[taro]]. The [[Hahajima]] district is the center of the pineapple industry, while the [[Toshima]] district produces premium sugarcane for the prefecture's [[Ogasawara rum]] distilleries.

The [[Ogasawara Pineapple]] (小笠原パイナップル) is a premium product marketed nationally for its exceptional sweetness and low acidity, attributes attributed to the volcanic soil and tropical sun. The [[Ogasawara Mango]] (小笠原マンゴー) is one of the most expensive fruits in Okaiken, with individual specimens selling for over 円10,000 in luxury department stores.

===Fishing and aquaculture===
The waters surrounding the Mujima Islands support rich fishing grounds, with the [[Kajika]] fishing fleet targeting [[mackerel]], [[tuna]], [[wahoo]], and [[mahimahi]]. The [[Kajika]] traditional fishing practices — including the {{nihongo|''tautai''|タウタイ|master fisherman}} system of reading ocean currents and fish behavior — are protected under the [[Kajika Cultural Preservation Act of 2005]] and are a source of both cultural pride and commercial advantage.

Aquaculture operations produce [[pearls]], [[oysters]], and [[sea grapes]] (緑海藻, ''midori-mōsō''), a tropical seaweed delicacy unique to the Mujima reefs.
As the southern maritime bastion of Ishijima, Ogasawara's deep-water port has become the primary operational hub for the [[Shendan Ocean Mining Authority]]. Specialized engineering facilities dock and maintain deep-sea robotic extraction platforms, automated benthic grabbers, and high-pressure submersibles capable of operating below four thousand meters along the Shendan Trench. The port's automated conveyor terminals process imported mineral sands and deep-water nodules, while naval drydocks service the patrol vessels and oceanographic survey fleets of the Okaiken Self-Defense Guard.

==Culture==
[[File:Ogasawara Festival.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Ogasawara Fire Festival, a fusion of Kajika, Vaiahi, and Sanese traditions held each August on Chichijima.]]

Ogasawara's culture is a Creole synthesis — a blending of [[Sanese]], [[Kajika]], [[Vaiahi]], [[Zong]]ese, and [[Yanbaru]] elements that has produced a distinctive identity found nowhere else in Okaiken. The prefecture's people are sometimes described as Okaiken's island cousins — a Polynesian-equivalent culture within the Sanese archipelago, where the communal values, oral traditions, and maritime identity of the Tautai peoples have merged with Sanese organizational structures and Zong culinary influences.

===The Kajika heritage===
The [[Kajika people]] are the indigenous population of the Mujima Islands and the dominant cultural force in the prefecture. Numbering approximately 22,000 within Ogasawara (and 1.14 million across the broader Ogasawara-Ishijima region), the Kajika maintain a vibrant cultural identity centered on [[traditional wayfinding]], [[vaka]] canoe construction, and the {{nihongo|''Puna Korero''|プナ・コレロ|"Source Stories"}} — the oral tradition that preserves the Kajika's migration history and navigation knowledge.

The [[Kajika language]], a Tautai language closely related to [[Vaiahi language|Vaiahi]], is spoken by approximately 18,500 people, most of whom are over 50. The language is critically endangered, but the [[Kajika Language Council]] and the {{nihongo|''Te Va''|テ・ヴァ|"The Space Between"}} advocacy organization lead preservation efforts, including immersion schools and wayfinding programs that teach navigation through the Kajika language.

===Cuisine===
Ogasawara's cuisine is the most distinctive in Okaiken — a tropical fusion that combines Sanese technique with Kajika ingredients, Vaiahi preparation methods, and Zong spice. The signature flavors are [[tropical fruit]], [[coconut]], [[Tosa pepper]], and [[fresh seafood]], creating a culinary tradition that is radically different from the rice-and-soy palate of the mainland.

The most famous dishes include:

* '''[[Ogasawara gekikara sōsu]]''' (小笠原激辛ソース) — The prefecture's signature hot sauce, a ferocious blend of Tosa pepper, mango, fermented fish, and tropical lime. The sauce is used on virtually everything and is Ogasawara's most successful food export, sold nationwide as a premium condiment.

* '''[[Ogasawara ramen]]''' (小笠原ラーメン) — A tropical ramen variant featuring a coconut-milk broth infused with Tosa pepper and mackerel stock, topped with grilled mahimahi, fresh mango salsa, and a drizzle of gekikara sōsu. The dish is a staple of the capital's ramen shops and has gained a cult following on the mainland.

* '''[[Vaka poke]]''' (ヴァカ・ポケ) — A raw fish salad of Kajika origin, similar to the Vaiahi ''oka'' but seasoned with Ogasawara hot sauce, coconut cream, and tropical citrus. The dish is served at the [[Ogasawara Seafood Market]] and at beachside stalls throughout the islands.

* '''[[Ogasawara rum]]''' (小笠原ラム) — A premium rum distilled from locally grown sugarcane, aged in tropical oak barrels. The [[Toshima]] distilleries produce several varieties, from light white rum to dark aged expressions that have won international awards.

* '''[[Hahajima pineapple cake]]''' (母島パイナップルケーキ) — A buttery pastry filled with fresh pineapple jam, sold as a souvenir and gift item throughout Okaiken.

===Festivals===
The [[Ogasawara Fire Festival]] (小笠原火祭り, ''Ogasawara-hi Matsuri''), held each August on Chichijima, is the prefecture's most important cultural event. The festival fuses Kajika fire-dancing traditions with Vaiahi ceremonial practices and Sanese [[matsuri]] elements, creating a three-day celebration of the islands' Creole identity. Fire dancers perform on the beach, vaka canoes race across the harbor, and the festival culminates in the {{nihongo|"Burning of the Sea"|海焼き|Umi-yaki}}, a ritual in which a flotilla of torch-lit canoes circles the harbor at sunset, setting the water ablaze with reflected light. The festival attracts over 80,000 visitors annually.

The [[Kajika Wayfinding Festival]] (カジカ・ウェイファインディング祭り, ''Kajika-weifaindingu Matsuri''), held each March, celebrates the traditional navigation heritage with vaka canoe races, wayfinding demonstrations, and a reenactment of the ancestral Tautai migration from the south.

===Music and dance===
Ogasawara's music is a fusion of Kajika drumming, Vaiahi harmonic singing, and Sanese pop influences. The {{nihongo|''tāmū''|ターム}} — a slit-drum ensemble of Kajika origin — provides the rhythmic foundation for most traditional performances, while the Vaiahi-influenced {{nihongo|''siva''|シバ}} dance tradition emphasizes fluid arm movements and hip oscillation that distinguish it sharply from the more rigid forms of mainland Sanese dance.

The prefecture has produced several nationally recognized musicians who blend the island's tropical sound with mainstream O-Pop, including the [[Ogasawara Roots]] band and the singer [[Lani Kajika]].

==Transportation==
[[File:Ogasawara Airport.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Ogasawara Airport, the primary air link between the Mujima Islands and the Okaiken mainland.]]

Ogasawara Prefecture is connected to the Okaiken mainland by air and sea. The [[Ogasawara Airport]] on Chichijima offers daily flights to [[Okami]], [[Aizawa]], and [[Toyozawa]], operated by [[Ogasawara Airlines]], a subsidiary of the national carrier. The flight from Okami takes approximately 2 hours and 15 minutes.

The [[Ogasawara Ferry]] operates a weekly overnight service from [[Aizawa]] to the Ogasawara Port on Chichijima, with a crossing time of approximately 24 hours. The ferry carries both passengers and cargo and is the primary means of transporting goods to the islands.

Inter-island transportation relies on the [[Mujima Maritime Transit Authority]], which operates high-speed catamarans connecting Chichijima, Hahajima, and the outer islands. The [[Kajika Navigation Service]], a community-operated enterprise, offers traditional vaka canoe transport between the closer islands — a service that is both practical and a tourist attraction.

The [[Ogasawara Monorail]], a 12-kilometre elevated line connecting the airport, the city center, and the hot springs district, is the only rail infrastructure in the prefecture.

==Education==
Ogasawara Prefecture is home to the [[Ogasawara Institute of Tropical Science]], a research institution specializing in tropical agriculture, marine biology, and volcanic geology. The institute operates experimental pineapple and sugarcane plantations on Hahajima and maintains the [[Ogasawara Coral Reef Observatory]], one of the most comprehensive reef monitoring stations in the Shendan basin.

The [[Kajika Wayfinding Academy]], a community-operated institution, trains navigators in the traditional Tautai wayfinding system, preserving knowledge that has been passed down orally for over two millennia.

